WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:06.230
Rollins professor at the Rollins School of
Public Health based there in Atlanta, Georgia

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:07.230
at Emory University.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:12.269
He is a member of the National Academy of
Science Committee on Applied and Theoretical

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:14.020
Aspects of Statistics.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:18.840
And his research involves the development
of statistical methods for geographic data,

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:24.039
including applications in environmental justice,
epidemiology, disease surveillance, spatial

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:27.940
cluster detection, conservation biology, and
disease ecology.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:33.590
His research has appeared in numerous biostatistical,
statistical, environmental health, and ecology

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:40.410
journals as well as he's authored a textbook
entitled Applied Spatial Statistics for Public

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:41.410
Health Data.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:48.750
And so prior to us getting Dr. Waller underway,
I'd like to invite you all to submit any questions

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:56.870
that you have for Dr. Waller through the GoToWebinar
question submission system, which is located

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:01.670
in the little console there off to, probably,
the right of your screen.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:05.439
And you can do that, and then we'll save some
time at the end where I'll read those questions

00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:07.649
off to Dr. Waller.

00:01:07.649 --> 00:01:11.259
So without further ado, Dr. Waller, the stage
is yours.

00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:12.259
Great.

00:01:12.259 --> 00:01:13.259
Thank you.

00:01:13.259 --> 00:01:17.649
It's a pleasure to be here, and I'd like to
offer my own wishes for a happy St. Patrick's

00:01:17.649 --> 00:01:18.649
Day.

00:01:18.649 --> 00:01:23.149
I hope you're are all doing very well in your
office basketball pools because, as data scientists,

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:24.729
you should be.

00:01:24.729 --> 00:01:29.159
Today we will be talking about-- well, that's
what I look like.

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:33.210
And we'll be talking about multiple testing,
bias, confounding, and missing data-- or as

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:37.349
a subtitle, what could go horribly wrong with
my analysis?

00:01:37.349 --> 00:01:43.350
I'm going to give a little insight on thinking
about team data science and team science and

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:48.210
the data science setting with a little bit
of background to distinguish between observational

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:52.840
experimental data and prediction versus estimation.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.979
To put this in context, I've been watching
these webinars over the last several weeks

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:02.450
to get a sense of the kinds of topics, and
we have a lot of people with a lot of different

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:03.450
interests.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:04.450
So I want to set some context.

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.560
We'll talk about multiple testing, bias, confounding,
missing data.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.840
I'll try to give an example for each and sources
for additional information and then wrap it

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.040
up coming back to team data science.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:20.290
So data science is often presented as requiring
skills from computer science, statistics,

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:25.390
or an application or subject matter area,
which often leads to the battle of the Venn

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:26.390
diagrams.

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:31.380
This is just one example here and a couple
of postings where people have looked at this,

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.400
but you can see the three scopes.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.470
We've got this pinkish color, hacking skills
or computer science.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.080
And we've got math and statistical knowledge.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.200
And then we've got the substance area.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.349
And in a school of public health, for me,
it's a lot of health-related research.

00:02:46.349 --> 00:02:51.260
Data science is, kind of, that sweet spot
in the middle.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.079
We could have more than three directions.

00:02:53.079 --> 00:02:57.530
One of them has a seven-set Venn diagram,
which I'd never seen before, so I thought

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:59.420
I'd share that with you too.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:04.230
The webinar series-- I really appreciate the
BD2K program putting this on because it has

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:08.540
provided and continues to provide a forum
for introduction across these elements.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.499
So if you go to data science seminars within
your own

00:03:11.499 --> 00:03:15.620
discipline, you tend to hear people with similar
perspectives talking.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.480
And, I think, really, where the growth is
is this cross-talk.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.900
So I've really learned a lot from the webinars
and hope you're enjoying them as well.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.849
In computer science, there have been webinars
on hardware-- just the basic setup of doing

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:28.889
big data.

00:03:28.889 --> 00:03:33.700
Also algorithms and statistics, we've got
several on concepts, techniques, Bayesian

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.410
analysis and so on.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:39.499
I think it's the intersection of all these
were the unique and emerging issues come up.

00:03:39.499 --> 00:03:45.030
And today what I'm talking about are trying
to draw some statistical concepts from observational

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:51.550
studies, often from epidemiology-- then how
these may scale up or how they may need to

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:53.580
be revised in a big data setting.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.810
So we'll talk about definitions, examples,
and the scaling issue primarily.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:03.560
I think each and every one of these is wide
open for additional work, so I'm not stating

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:08.480
that these are problems of a statistical nature
and dropping the microphone, but, instead,

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.200
we want to have an invitation for the interdisciplinary
work and really work those intersections in

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.280
the Venn diagram.

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:21.060
Team data science-- there's a lot of interesting
discussions going on at conferences and in

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:24.449
publications on the intersection between these
areas.

00:04:24.449 --> 00:04:28.500
And one thing that I've heard recently is
the discussion of whether a team of data scientists

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:34.050
should consist of several people who are identical
or several people with complementary skills.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:38.620
I tend to favor the idea of the heterogeneous
team because it brings different expertise

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:42.300
and experience, also different problem solving
intuition.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.900
That's a big deal to me.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.090
Are people understanding?

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:46.389
How do they approach a problem?

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:48.500
What do they think the cool part of it is?

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:51.990
If you have different people on the team looking
at that from different aspects, I think you

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:58.620
get much more creative, analytic, and interpretation
perspectives in your analysis.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:02.810
Interdisciplinary team science can be very
tricky if you want to go all the way to transdisciplinary

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:04.810
collaborations.

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:09.270
I tell my students and colleagues that I think
it's a lot like international travel when

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.840
you work interdisciplinary setting.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:12.889
The language is different.

00:05:12.889 --> 00:05:14.120
The social norms are different.

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:15.860
What the cool journals are is different.

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:21.620
How money counts, what you should write into
a grant-- that all needs to be negotiated.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:26.060
It's also a lot like a middle school dance
where you've got multiple groups who are interested

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:29.699
and they know they want to interact with each
other but they're not quite sure how to do

00:05:29.699 --> 00:05:30.699
that yet.

00:05:30.699 --> 00:05:34.440
And so they tend to cover up the insecurity
with a lot of teasing.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:40.639
And we see that in the publications, too,
of-- they're trying to establish their own

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:43.509
discipline as the key discipline in here.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:48.580
I also, recently-- I don't have this on the
slide-- heard a definition that disciplinary

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:53.389
work is like an apple.

00:05:53.389 --> 00:05:57.060
Disciplinary collaboration's like a fruit
basket.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.500
Interdisciplinary works like a fruit salad.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:02.460
And transdisciplinary work is like a smoothie.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.690
So how to data impact science?

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:05.690
That's our question.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:10.890
Our goal and big data to knowledge is to take
data, extract information from it and develop

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:12.069
knowledge.

00:06:12.069 --> 00:06:16.690
So I want to frame this in terms of questions,
analytic tools, and answers.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:20.729
And this is something we present in class
a lot-- the geographic information systems

00:06:20.729 --> 00:06:22.199
class I start with.

00:06:22.199 --> 00:06:23.759
And I can't take credit for this.

00:06:23.759 --> 00:06:28.729
John Richardson, who was a toxicologist at
the EPA Region 4 office here in Atlanta once

00:06:28.729 --> 00:06:29.729
said, it's this simple.

00:06:29.729 --> 00:06:31.129
And he drew this picture.

00:06:31.129 --> 00:06:33.440
And I thought it was brilliant, and I really
like this.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.259
So we start in the upper left.

00:06:35.259 --> 00:06:37.150
There's some question we want to answer.

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:45.350
I know in a lot of unstructured data mining
there's a lot of talk about hypothesis-free

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:48.080
analysis, but I don't think we go in completely
blind.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:50.450
I think there are things we're interested
in finding out.

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:55.300
So they may be hard to articulate, but this
first step is articulating what you're looking

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.379
for, what kinds of things you're looking for.

00:06:57.379 --> 00:07:01.939
And before running anything or before extracting
data or looking for the data, I think it's

00:07:01.939 --> 00:07:05.940
important to spend some time thinking about
what data what you really need to answer that

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:07.900
question completely.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:10.680
I do a lot of environmental health analysis.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:16.020
So if I were interested in what's the impact
of exposure to drinking water near a hazardous

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:20.120
waste site on childhood leukemia rates, I
might want to know where every child lives

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.570
at what age, how much water they drink, does
their house require this?

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:26.979
I could go through a list of all the stuff
I'd love to get that I probably wouldn't be

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:27.979
able to.

00:07:27.979 --> 00:07:32.610
But that list is a helpful mental checklist
when you take the next step, and say what

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:35.289
data are available?

00:07:35.289 --> 00:07:39.280
We have a wide variety of data types available
to us now.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:42.699
So we can get all sorts of things, but we
rarely get everything we want.

00:07:42.699 --> 00:07:45.169
And we often get proxies for things we want.

00:07:45.169 --> 00:07:50.199
Like, I may not know which child is drinking
from which water system, but I know where

00:07:50.199 --> 00:07:53.470
they live, and I know where the hazardous
waste site is.

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:58.569
And I also may be able to find out that that
address is listed on the county water or public

00:07:58.569 --> 00:07:59.569
water bill.

00:07:59.569 --> 00:08:03.590
So I know they're getting water to the house,
but I don't know exactly what's going inside.

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:06.400
So it tells me something but not everything.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:10.780
So on the lower, right-hand side, I get the
data I can get and the methods I have available.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:14.510
I do answer some questions as I move up to
the upper right.

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:18.330
There are questions I can answer with my data
and my methods.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:22.719
And, probably, the most important step here
is to go back to the beginning and see how

00:08:22.719 --> 00:08:27.520
close the question I answered is to the question
I want to answer.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:30.870
And I've presented this to many different
classes.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:33.870
And one of the most interesting questions
I got was a student asking me, well, how do

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:36.170
you know when you're done?

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:40.000
And my answer was, well, I've been doing this
for 25, 30 years.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:43.050
And, so far, we've never finished any of those.

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:46.690
You get around once, and you think about what
the next step is.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:51.180
So you keep refining and getting closer and
closer pushing those two together.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.230
But I don't know that it's ever complete.

00:08:53.230 --> 00:08:58.680
This is just the research cycle and the way
I think we want to think about our data analysis.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:03.620
So I also want to go into this pointing out
that our perspective as an individual data

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:07.560
scientist can impact what we see in the data.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:09.130
Data science can reveal new things.

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:14.420
So we may have some really cool visualizations
or we hook together data that haven't been

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:15.420
together.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:19.930
But we also have to be open to seeing the
patterns or the results that come out.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:23.360
So we can link and process, wrangle, and munge
our data, but what does it really mean?

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.520
So I'm going to give a data science example
from 1854.

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:32.570
During the cholera outbreak in London-- this
was before the germ theory of disease was

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:36.660
well-established, so there were the miasma
people who thought it was in the air.

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:41.830
You could smell how bad it was in the summer,
and that's when it broke out-- versus contagion

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:42.830
ideas.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:49.600
The miasmist theory at the time was that there
was bad air in the storm sewer system.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:51.730
It was coming out through the drain holes.

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:55.583
And then, as many of you may be familiar with
John Snow's work, he was thinking that it

00:09:55.583 --> 00:09:57.670
was the water from the water pumps.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:00.310
So this is big data circa 1854.

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:06.940
This is a chart in a book referenced on the
right, Cartographies of Disease, of the cholera

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:10.050
outbreak in 1854 presented to the London Board
of Health.

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:15.160
And I'm not sure if you can see my cursor,
but this big, curved spike on the bottom is

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:18.850
the number of cases of cholera along with
diarrheal disease.

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:21.320
Those who follow each other carefully.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:25.550
Everything in the background are meteorological
things, like barometric pressure and rainfall

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:28.501
and temperature and humidity.

00:10:28.501 --> 00:10:31.920
And they were doing, on this chart, a lot
of what we try to do on the computer now--

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:36.970
is try to line up what's the magic signal
that starts off the outbreak.

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:42.580
So some of the things we're doing-- even though
it feels very modern, this sort of scientific

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:48.530
inquisitiveness with when you're presented
with lots of data from lots of sources-- has

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:50.040
been around for a while.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:56.660
Of course, the big news in 1854 was Dr. John
Snow, an anesthesiologist who mapped the cholera

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:58.030
deaths in a neighborhood.

00:10:58.030 --> 00:10:59.470
And I apologize in advance.

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:03.360
This slide has sound that was attached to
it by Carol Gotway, my co-author on our book,

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:06.410
and I haven't been able to figure out how
to get it out.

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:11.910
But the way the story generally goes is Snow
maps the cases in this particular neighborhood

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:12.910
in London.

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:13.910
He looks at that map.

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:19.270
He sees the concentration of the deaths around
a particular water pump on Broad Street, and

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:20.370
says, eureka!

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:22.100
That must be to cause.

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:27.042
Snow led an effort with others with the London
Board of Health to remove the handle of that

00:11:27.042 --> 00:11:28.960
particular water pump.

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:33.300
So the short version of this story is Snow
did phenomenal work, and reading some of his

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:36.830
papers is highly recommended.

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:40.230
But this has been, kind of, compressed into
a fairy tale.

00:11:40.230 --> 00:11:45.920
So this is a travel log for London saying,
"In 1854 Londoners were dropping like flies

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:49.060
from cholera until Dr. Snow figured out that
the bacteria were in the water."

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:50.990
He turned the pump off, saved countless lives.

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:53.160
And now, there's the John Snow pub nearby.

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.230
So that's good information.

00:11:56.230 --> 00:12:01.810
But it does compress a lot of work that was
going on and a lot of controversy.

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:03.870
So we look at this visualization of the map.

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:08.390
John Snow's map's often presented as-- good
grief, if you just map the data, look at what

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:10.070
you can find.

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:14.700
And so it looks like a real triumph of 1854
data science and visualization.

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:19.520
But if you look carefully, the maps of the
miasmists looked exactly like Dr. Snow's.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:23.070
And if you think about where the drain holes
are, they're right by the water pumps, 'cause

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:26.520
if your pump leaks, you'd like to have it
drain out.

00:12:26.520 --> 00:12:27.560
So the maps look the same.

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:32.990
And, in fact, one of the leaders of the London
Board of Health had the following response

00:12:32.990 --> 00:12:34.440
to Snow's 1854 analysis.

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:38.620
And he says, "This certainly looks more like
the effect of that atmospheric cause--" or

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:40.440
miasma-- "than any other.

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:44.050
If it were in the water, why wouldn't the
cholera have prevailed equally everywhere

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:46.100
where" people were drinking the water?

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:49.820
So we've got two different groups of people
looking at the same data, the same visualizations,

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:52.500
and they come to a different conclusion.

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:56.820
Now history supports Snow's conclusion, and
we see that as a triumph.

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:02.620
But it wasn't as if he slammed the map down
on the table and everybody saw the truth.

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:03.620
So that's a nice story.

00:13:03.620 --> 00:13:06.010
What does this mean for us?

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:10.550
A lot of data-- there's been a lot of data
science breakthroughs in managing, linking,

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:12.400
processing, and visualizing distributed data.

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:17.180
But you could think about what would someone
like John Snow, an 1850s data scientist, do

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:18.180
with modern computing?

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:23.170
Like, if he had a geographic information system
or Google or social media, well, you might

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:26.820
be thinking he would be something like Google
Flu Trends.

00:13:26.820 --> 00:13:31.300
Google Flu Trends' prediction goal was to
try to beat the CDC's projection of number

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:34.520
of influenza-like illnesses over a week.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:37.600
These were reported weekly.

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:39.110
And they were doing it for a while.

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:43.470
They were about two weeks ahead of the CDC
release numbers.

00:13:43.470 --> 00:13:47.680
These are no longer published on a regular
basis, although you can still get them.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:53.370
So what happened is in February of 2013, the
Google Flu Trends was tweaking its algorithm--

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:58.200
or, actually, a month before that-- and started
to overpredict.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:03.370
And in a particular week in February, they
predicted twice as many cases as actually

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:04.630
showed up.

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:06.070
So there's a very nice summary of this.

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:11.700
It's a short article on science by Lazer et
al-"The Parable of Google Flu."

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:15.760
And I spelled flu incorrectly, or it was auto-corrected.

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:22.160
So that's, I, guess another bit of big data
for you.

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:25.550
The way Google Flu Trends works-- it's based
correlated searches.

00:14:25.550 --> 00:14:30.040
And so you're looking for correlations between
the kind of searches that happen that correspond

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:37.150
to the beginning or intensification of flu
outbreaks and things like high school basketball.

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:40.020
Searches for high school basketball score
will start at the beginning of the winter

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:41.670
about the same time the flu starts.

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:46.130
So it's not a causal connection, but it does
have strong predictive power.

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:52.090
And this has been one of the big successes
of Google and other data science development

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:53.090
areas.

00:14:53.090 --> 00:15:00.740
Now that the Flu Trends algorithm was proprietary
and consistently tweaked, and so it's hard

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:03.590
to say that it was wrong all along.

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:04.590
That's not true.

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:05.590
It did very well.

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:08.920
And I'll show you one of the plots from the
Lazer paper on the

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:10.280
next slide.

00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:14.640
And the authors don't discount the impact
of big data.

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:20.230
What they do discount is-- or point out--
that it offers an example of big data hubris.

00:15:20.230 --> 00:15:25.490
Or we ignore everything we know about flu,
and just go with the data.

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:30.720
So this avoids the subject matter expertise
part of the Venn diagram and starts to wander,

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:36.010
maybe, not the part labeled danger zone, but
it starts to get away from as good a job as

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:37.560
we could do.

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:43.860
So this example, to me, is an example against
the idea of totally hypothesis-free science.

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:47.520
And I'm sure those people will debate me on
that.

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:51.250
And I think we need to worry about hypothesis
and past knowledges that we know.

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:56.400
I don't know that we have to identify a testable
hypothesis for everything we do, but we certainly

00:15:56.400 --> 00:16:01.500
need to be documenting what we're after and
how we plan to find it.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:03.670
This is the figure showing the estimates.

00:16:03.670 --> 00:16:10.830
And what you see in the top plot is the percent
of influenza-like illnesses.

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:18.170
And the bottom is just the error between the
two, showing the noise around zero, and what

00:16:18.170 --> 00:16:19.980
you see is starting after.

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:27.790
After July 2011, it starts to deviate some
more and into '13.

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:31.820
So with things like that, if we find a mistake,
it's not so much pointing out a mistake and

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:34.280
saying that it's wrong.

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:35.790
It's what could we do better with this?

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:38.210
So there's several quotes about learning from
mistakes.

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:42.590
You know, anyone who's never made a mistake
has never learned anything new.

00:16:42.590 --> 00:16:44.210
Each mistake should teach us something.

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:47.310
And one of my recent favorites is always make
new mistakes.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:52.080
It's-- you should learn from the mistakes
you make, but don't be afraid to try new things.

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:57.300
And so how do we expand data science without
reinventing the wheel regarding data challenges?

00:16:57.300 --> 00:16:59.720
Some of that is knowledge and summaries of
these.

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:05.199
So in order to avoid mistakes in data science
as we expand, we should carry an appreciation

00:17:05.199 --> 00:17:09.630
for data-based stumbling blocks that have
been established in the past and explore how

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:15.260
these manifest at scale and keep an eye out
for new sorts of challenges that may not have

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:18.949
been an issue in older-size datasets but would
be now.

00:17:18.949 --> 00:17:23.850
So I'm going to review four data analysis
issues that remain relevant for big data but

00:17:23.850 --> 00:17:27.699
also need to be customized and refined in
the big data context.

00:17:27.699 --> 00:17:31.190
There's a couple of important contrasts I
want to mention too.

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:35.179
The first is observational versus experimental
data.

00:17:35.179 --> 00:17:39.420
And this is where you're taking data that
you are seeing versus data that comes from

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:40.710
a designed experiment.

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:45.049
There was a period of time where the literature
referred to this as found data.

00:17:45.049 --> 00:17:48.049
Working in geographic information systems,
we do this a lot.

00:17:48.049 --> 00:17:49.350
We'll take the census data.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:53.010
I don't want to map the census regions because
they've already done that.

00:17:53.010 --> 00:17:58.179
I don't need to re-establish the census data,
but I want to link it to new data.

00:17:58.179 --> 00:18:02.570
And a message I tend to give my students is
the data always looks greener if you didn't

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:03.669
collect them.

00:18:03.669 --> 00:18:06.149
I think we
think that about social media data.

00:18:06.149 --> 00:18:11.259
If you think about electronic medical records,
they include a lot of information, but some

00:18:11.259 --> 00:18:12.820
of it may be opinion.

00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:17.159
Some of it may not be as good as it sounds.

00:18:17.159 --> 00:18:24.149
So as we grab the observational data to make
decisions, one question is does design matter?

00:18:24.149 --> 00:18:27.190
Should we be hoping for an experiment or link
this to it?

00:18:27.190 --> 00:18:28.190
Does it even matter?

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:34.139
I think I gave a big data talk once where
my title was Design is Dead, Long Live Design.

00:18:34.139 --> 00:18:38.400
And my argument was we needed to come up with
new types of design to understand the sorts

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:40.029
of studies we were doing.

00:18:40.029 --> 00:18:45.940
And that doesn't mean not to take use of the
big data, but it's to try to figure out how

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:48.690
to make the best use of big data.

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:52.669
So some questions-- do data have to come from
a design to be useful?

00:18:52.669 --> 00:18:57.110
I don't think that's necessarily the case,
but I think you can gain things from a design.

00:18:57.110 --> 00:19:00.240
And does data quality matter if I can get
a lot of data?

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:02.200
This is another big argument.

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:05.299
If I'd like independent observations, that'd
be great.

00:19:05.299 --> 00:19:09.830
But I could take a whole lot of correlated
ones and, maybe, get the same information.

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:12.510
So what do you lose in observational data?

00:19:12.510 --> 00:19:18.779
At a smaller scale, this is something epidemiologists
have been concerned with for a long time.

00:19:18.779 --> 00:19:23.080
One design data about-- I thought this was
a really nice example that Xiao-Li Meng from

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:24.080
Harvard.

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:25.080
has put together.

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:27.029
And he proposes an interesting thought experiment.

00:19:27.029 --> 00:19:32.659
Now this is in a discussion-- the reference
is on the bottom-- of a paper by Keiding and

00:19:32.659 --> 00:19:37.909
Louis on "The Perils and Pitfalls of Self-selected
Entry into Epidemiological Studies and Surveys--"

00:19:37.909 --> 00:19:43.940
the idea of using web forms, web recruitment,
to define your control base and so on.

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:45.309
And it raises a lot of question.

00:19:45.309 --> 00:19:48.190
And there are many pages of discussion after.

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:51.799
So if that's an area you're interested in,
I think there's a lot of good information

00:19:51.799 --> 00:19:53.840
in the article and the discussion.

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.159
But Xiao-Li points out this example.

00:19:56.159 --> 00:19:59.549
Suppose we're interested in estimating the
average income in the United States, and we

00:19:59.549 --> 00:20:01.379
had two choices of data sets.

00:20:01.379 --> 00:20:05.490
One was 100 individuals selected in a simple
random sample.

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:10.039
So every individual in the United States is
equally likely to be selected and would participate.

00:20:10.039 --> 00:20:15.860
Or we could get data from 50 million individuals,
but it's not as clean data that there's a

00:20:15.860 --> 00:20:21.330
correlation between their income, which we're
interested in, and the probability of being

00:20:21.330 --> 00:20:22.330
selected.

00:20:22.330 --> 00:20:23.330
It's not a big correlation.

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:26.019
And he was setting an example of 0.05.

00:20:26.019 --> 00:20:29.769
So what we're saying is suppose income was
slightly correlated with the probability of

00:20:29.769 --> 00:20:33.860
completing a web survey about your income,
and then you want to get the estimate.

00:20:33.860 --> 00:20:36.899
Well, 50 million in a hundred seems like,
wow, that's so big.

00:20:36.899 --> 00:20:38.769
That must be good enough.

00:20:38.769 --> 00:20:39.990
And Meng does some algebra.

00:20:39.990 --> 00:20:41.419
I'm not going to show you.

00:20:41.419 --> 00:20:45.799
But it's in the discussion that there's actually
more information in the small sample.

00:20:45.799 --> 00:20:50.259
You'd need about 62 million to get the same
amount of information.

00:20:50.259 --> 00:20:53.010
So in a big
data setting, we do get a lot more.

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:58.370
But I think there's a lot of interesting calculations
like this one to be done to talk about the

00:20:58.370 --> 00:21:04.470
value and quantity of information contained
in data like that.

00:21:04.470 --> 00:21:08.889
Also in design versus experiment, design experiments
allow one to control outside variables-- things

00:21:08.889 --> 00:21:14.169
like temperature or genetic strain or precipitation--
that could have an impact on your outcome,

00:21:14.169 --> 00:21:15.940
but they're not your primary interest.

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:19.499
And in studying flu, say, if we only took
observations in the summer, we would miss

00:21:19.499 --> 00:21:24.610
things like the peak that generally happens
in the winter, associations with travel periods

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:29.029
and the times when school starts.

00:21:29.029 --> 00:21:34.570
Design can help remove things, but we also
don't want to design our way around important

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:37.220
contributors.

00:21:37.220 --> 00:21:41.220
So we have observational versus experimental
data.

00:21:41.220 --> 00:21:42.220
We'll be talking mostly about observational.

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:46.179
The second contrast is prediction versus estimation.

00:21:46.179 --> 00:21:50.230
I'm not going to make a contrast in my conclusions,
but I do want to point out these are different

00:21:50.230 --> 00:21:51.919
issues, and we'll bring up both.

00:21:51.919 --> 00:21:57.600
A lot of this discussion hinges on a paper
in 2001 on the two cultures in statistical

00:21:57.600 --> 00:22:02.700
modeling that I think is one of the things
that feeds into data science.

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:05.660
The basic idea is if we have input, these
might be predictors.

00:22:05.660 --> 00:22:07.320
They go into a box.

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:10.149
Something happens in that box, and we observe
some output.

00:22:10.149 --> 00:22:12.580
The two cultures differ in what we're interested
in.

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:16.970
The prediction group is-- given me a new x,
can I predict y really well?

00:22:16.970 --> 00:22:18.710
And I don't really care what happens in the
box.

00:22:18.710 --> 00:22:21.669
I just want to be able to predict the outcome
well.

00:22:21.669 --> 00:22:25.980
This information side, or estimation side,
is can I define the associations between x

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:29.259
and y and try to figure out what's happening
inside the box?

00:22:29.259 --> 00:22:33.679
That may be just measures of associations,
like regressions and logistic regressions,

00:22:33.679 --> 00:22:40.320
or it may well be something like more advanced
machine learning, deep learning, or a causal

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:41.539
inference.

00:22:41.539 --> 00:22:44.789
So the distinction between the two is what
do you care to find out?

00:22:44.789 --> 00:22:46.920
In epidemiology, there's a little bit of both.

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:51.520
We do want to predict how many flu cases there
are, because one, it can tell us how to set

00:22:51.520 --> 00:22:53.010
up a prevention scheme.

00:22:53.010 --> 00:22:57.580
If we know the types of strains, it can help
us plan for vaccinations.

00:22:57.580 --> 00:23:02.070
But we're also-- there's a medical interest
in what causes things to spread.

00:23:02.070 --> 00:23:07.260
And we're interested in what happens inside
the box too because if we predict well, I'd

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:11.700
like to know what's in the box so I could
change the outcome.

00:23:11.700 --> 00:23:14.269
So what can get in the way of doing either
prediction or estimation?

00:23:14.269 --> 00:23:16.100
How do we get the most out of big data?

00:23:16.100 --> 00:23:20.679
Well, with respect to prediction-- again,
in Google Flu Trends, it's, like, we didn't

00:23:20.679 --> 00:23:25.230
really care how basketball scores were associated
with flu, but we found out they helped us

00:23:25.230 --> 00:23:26.230
predict better.

00:23:26.230 --> 00:23:27.499
So that's a good thing.

00:23:27.499 --> 00:23:31.090
With estimation, what can get in the way is
what makes our estimates wrong.

00:23:31.090 --> 00:23:34.309
And then, if they're biased, is bias
always a bad thing?

00:23:34.309 --> 00:23:35.970
So I'll come back to that in a minute.

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:40.120
These are the four issues we're going to be
talking about in this context of big data

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:46.580
and data science-- multiple testing, bias,
confounding, and missing and mismeasured data.

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:51.450
So multiple testing-- and statistical hypothesis
testing's been a mainstay for small data analysis

00:23:51.450 --> 00:23:52.450
for decades.

00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:53.450
Now there's been a lot written about it.

00:23:53.450 --> 00:23:57.740
It certainly has-- there are some legitimate
concerns about turning all of your results

00:23:57.740 --> 00:24:00.450
down into a yes or no answer.

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:01.559
But it's still out there.

00:24:01.559 --> 00:24:07.679
The P-value reports is certainly something
to discuss, but you will see a lot of publications

00:24:07.679 --> 00:24:09.129
based on that.

00:24:09.129 --> 00:24:14.129
So in a statistical hypothesis testing setting,
you reject the null hypothesis at some level

00:24:14.129 --> 00:24:16.850
of statistical significance, and typically,
this is 0.05.

00:24:16.850 --> 00:24:21.700
And there's probably a lot of talks about
why 5% is there.

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:25.909
So this means for each test there's a 5% chance
of rejecting the null hypothesis when the

00:24:25.909 --> 00:24:27.860
null hypothesis is actually true.

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:29.769
So it's a false positive result.

00:24:29.769 --> 00:24:32.269
Well, it's 5% for each test.

00:24:32.269 --> 00:24:35.360
But if you're doing a lot of tests, what does
this mean?

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:39.369
So when we're testing a lot of hypotheses--
and these might be checking associations between

00:24:39.369 --> 00:24:44.570
several outcomes or several variables-- you
might think of a gene expression microarray,

00:24:44.570 --> 00:24:46.470
lots and lots of observations.

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:50.380
If the tests were independent of one another,
then you would expect one in 20 to yield a

00:24:50.380 --> 00:24:53.669
false positive result at this 0.05 level.

00:24:53.669 --> 00:24:56.169
But what if you're testing 200,000 genes?

00:24:56.169 --> 00:25:01.450
The magnitude of the problem-- there's a lot
of highly technical statistical calculations

00:25:01.450 --> 00:25:06.710
you can do on this to get exactly what it
is, but-- especially if you've got correlation

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:07.710
and not independence.

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:09.820
But the problem remains how you scale this
up.

00:25:09.820 --> 00:25:11.519
So what do you do?

00:25:11.519 --> 00:25:15.940
Some potential solutions-- and some of which
have been around for a long time-- there's

00:25:15.940 --> 00:25:17.190
Bonferroni adjustment.

00:25:17.190 --> 00:25:21.100
If you have k independent tests, you just
adjust the significance level of each of the

00:25:21.100 --> 00:25:29.460
tests by dividing by k, so the overall probability
of finding any false positives is 5%.

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:31.740
So it's a very conservative adjustment.

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:32.890
And it doesn't really scale well.

00:25:32.890 --> 00:25:40.470
So if I want to split up my 0.05 across 200,000
tests, I'm talking about a P-value for significance

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:43.389
of 0.00000025. 0 And that's not particularly
meaningful.

00:25:43.389 --> 00:25:47.169
You think about how much data you would have
to collect to have that kind of precision

00:25:47.169 --> 00:25:52.299
out in the tails to be sure that that's what
your probability was.

00:25:52.299 --> 00:25:56.700
Putting these things together usually gives
a family-wise probability of any false discoveries.

00:25:56.700 --> 00:26:00.899
The probability that the number of false discoveries
is bigger than zero.

00:26:00.899 --> 00:26:03.749
And that's served pretty well.

00:26:03.749 --> 00:26:06.809
There's a lot of literature on multiple comparisons.

00:26:06.809 --> 00:26:12.190
My own father wrote his dissertation on multiple
comparison issues, and there's a SAS procedure

00:26:12.190 --> 00:26:14.909
with a Waller option using his technique.

00:26:14.909 --> 00:26:18.110
But their scalability is a real problem here.

00:26:18.110 --> 00:26:24.990
In 1995, and refined in 2000, Benjamini and
Hochberg introduced a concept called the False

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:29.010
Discovery Rate,
rather than the probability of false discoveries.

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:32.850
Now we often think of rates and probabilities
as similar, but there is a key distinction

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:38.100
here that they were making-- that false discovery
rate's not the probability of any false discovery.

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:44.379
It's the expected fraction of discoveries
you make, or any cutoff, that are false.

00:26:44.379 --> 00:26:50.190
So this is different than what the Bonferroni
family-wise is doing, which is the probability

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:54.330
of having any false discoveries being bigger
than zero is 0.05.

00:26:54.330 --> 00:27:00.000
So Benjamini and Hochberg are saying, we think
it would be worse to have four false discoveries

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:03.679
out of 10 than having 20 out of 100, where
that'd be one fifth.

00:27:03.679 --> 00:27:08.070
So it's the rate of out of how many you detect,
what fraction do you think are false?

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:13.150
And this is fundamentally a different way
of thinking about it but one that scales much,

00:27:13.150 --> 00:27:14.150
much better.

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:17.700
And so there's been widespread use of the
false discovery rate, especially in the genetics

00:27:17.700 --> 00:27:18.950
and genomics literature.

00:27:18.950 --> 00:27:23.519
And there's a lot of resources for exactly
how to calculate the false discovery rate.

00:27:23.519 --> 00:27:27.360
I'm not going to talk about those here today,
so we can talk about the other topics.

00:27:27.360 --> 00:27:32.970
But it's an excellent illustration of how
an inferential idea-things like false discoveries

00:27:32.970 --> 00:27:39.429
and type I error rates-- needed to be rethought
to scale to the big data setting.

00:27:39.429 --> 00:27:43.309
So we had an adequate solution at small scale,
but it didn't extend.

00:27:43.309 --> 00:27:48.789
So addressing the issue required backing up
a little bit to that in the whirling vortex,

00:27:48.789 --> 00:27:53.129
what question do we really want to answer
here, and starting from those ideas again.

00:27:53.129 --> 00:27:59.139
There's a really nice FDR overview and some
slides that Chris Genovese has posted that

00:27:59.139 --> 00:28:04.179
I used to help summarize some of the results
above.

00:28:04.179 --> 00:28:07.220
So moving on to bias from the multiple testing.

00:28:07.220 --> 00:28:12.150
The quintessential example of anyone teaching
bias and variability- I know this is the case

00:28:12.150 --> 00:28:16.221
in public health and, probably, a lot of areas--
is this four targets here.

00:28:16.221 --> 00:28:18.000
And you're shooting arrows at the target.

00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:21.440
Upper left, we've got low bias and low variability.

00:28:21.440 --> 00:28:24.749
We average out there in the bull's eye, and
they're not very far apart.

00:28:24.749 --> 00:28:29.030
Moving to the right, we're still averaging
out near the bullseye, but there's a lot more

00:28:29.030 --> 00:28:30.030
spread.

00:28:30.030 --> 00:28:32.980
And a single shot may be further away.

00:28:32.980 --> 00:28:35.559
Bottom left, we've got high bias and low variability.

00:28:35.559 --> 00:28:39.830
We keep hitting the same spot, but we're off
consistently by some amount.

00:28:39.830 --> 00:28:43.389
And the last one, we're not close to the bull's
eye, and we're not very consistent- so high

00:28:43.389 --> 00:28:44.950
bias, high variability.

00:28:44.950 --> 00:28:48.970
So this connection with bias and variability
is an important one I'd like to keep talking

00:28:48.970 --> 00:28:49.970
about.

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:53.450
But first, this is The New Yorker cartoon
version of that.

00:28:53.450 --> 00:28:57.940
There's something called the Texas Sharpshooter
approach where you shoot the side of the barn

00:28:57.940 --> 00:28:59.210
then paint the bull's eye around it.

00:28:59.210 --> 00:29:04.970
This is the medieval version where you've
got someone to do the painting for you.

00:29:04.970 --> 00:29:06.889
So some bias we live with every day.

00:29:06.889 --> 00:29:10.450
So those of you who are parents probably have
used an ear thermometer.

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:13.600
Or those of you who just don't like having
a thermometer under your tongue may have used

00:29:13.600 --> 00:29:14.600
an ear thermometer.

00:29:14.600 --> 00:29:17.320
They don't give you the same number.

00:29:17.320 --> 00:29:21.879
They both measure body temperature, and they're
both pretty reliable.

00:29:21.879 --> 00:29:24.080
They have smaller variability.

00:29:24.080 --> 00:29:27.970
And you get the similar answers as long as
you use it right, but they're not centered

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:28.970
in the same place.

00:29:28.970 --> 00:29:31.179
In this case, there's low variability.

00:29:31.179 --> 00:29:35.679
You've got reproduceable bias, but we can
correct for the bias with a simple adjustment.

00:29:35.679 --> 00:29:40.429
In the case of the thermometers, we memorize
what normal is on either one.

00:29:40.429 --> 00:29:43.429
And then if you take your child's temperature,
you're also trying to remember is this-- do

00:29:43.429 --> 00:29:46.440
they seem well enough to go to school or not.

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:48.000
Bias and variability come together.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:53.619
And there's this bias variability trade-off
in a lot of statistics books.

00:29:53.619 --> 00:29:57.270
And one of these is if you're talking about
the mean squared error prediction, how well

00:29:57.270 --> 00:29:58.320
are you doing?

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:03.019
One summary of that is taking the expected
value of the difference between your prediction

00:30:03.019 --> 00:30:06.179
and the truth, what you're predicting, squaring
that.

00:30:06.179 --> 00:30:07.980
That's called the mean square error.

00:30:07.980 --> 00:30:11.489
And doing some algebra, you find this is a
sum of two things.

00:30:11.489 --> 00:30:16.210
If the variance of your prediction plus the
square of the bias of your prediction, how

00:30:16.210 --> 00:30:17.210
far you're off.

00:30:17.210 --> 00:30:22.190
So if bias goes down, variance can go up for
the mean squared error can still stay the

00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:23.190
same.

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:26.940
So the accuracy of your prediction is a function
of both the variability of your prediction

00:30:26.940 --> 00:30:28.690
and the squared bias and your prediction.

00:30:28.690 --> 00:30:33.090
This is an old statistical idea, and it carries
a lot of settings.

00:30:33.090 --> 00:30:36.929
And sometimes, you can reduce the variance
by incorporating a little bias.

00:30:36.929 --> 00:30:43.049
So Tom Lewis has a nice essay on bias isn't
necessarily a bad thing as long as you have

00:30:43.049 --> 00:30:46.379
some control over it, if it helps rein things
in.

00:30:46.379 --> 00:30:50.659
This is an element of a lot of Bayesian methods
which induce some bias, but they actually

00:30:50.659 --> 00:30:54.590
add precision, and they borrow information
across data sets.

00:30:54.590 --> 00:31:00.129
I think, last week, Michael Newton's BD2K
seminar in Bayesian statistics gave some overview

00:31:00.129 --> 00:31:01.129
of this.

00:31:01.129 --> 00:31:07.850
But if you look up borrowing information,
you'll see how a biased estimate can actually

00:31:07.850 --> 00:31:10.029
give you some improvement over an unbiased
one.

00:31:10.029 --> 00:31:12.220
Let's think of an example of linear regression.

00:31:12.220 --> 00:31:17.529
A lot of times when you are taught linear
regression, you're told that ordinary least

00:31:17.529 --> 00:31:20.429
squares estimates are unbiased.

00:31:20.429 --> 00:31:24.570
And in ordinary least squares, you're trying
to pick some parameter's data to minimize

00:31:24.570 --> 00:31:32.149
the difference between the regression model,
the x beta minus the observations y squared.

00:31:32.149 --> 00:31:33.149
And it's unbiased.

00:31:33.149 --> 00:31:34.710
But there's an important caveat to that.

00:31:34.710 --> 00:31:38.299
It's only unbiased if we have all the right
covariates in the model.

00:31:38.299 --> 00:31:43.019
And I put this simple regression picture up
here that I made up because when I took a

00:31:43.019 --> 00:31:46.530
regression class, they told us how to build
a least square machine.

00:31:46.530 --> 00:31:48.049
I thought this was a cool idea.

00:31:48.049 --> 00:31:51.279
It would be hard to scale this up, though.

00:31:51.279 --> 00:31:56.360
But if I had a board of wood, and I nailed
in nails were all my points were, and I had

00:31:56.360 --> 00:32:00.080
a stick, and I had rubber bands from each
point-- and I have to make sure the rubber

00:32:00.080 --> 00:32:02.499
bands are going up and down with the y direction.

00:32:02.499 --> 00:32:08.509
But you snap it, the stick, the equilibrium
point where all the pressure is on, the stick

00:32:08.509 --> 00:32:10.529
will land on the least squares regression
line.

00:32:10.529 --> 00:32:11.529
So let's think about that.

00:32:11.529 --> 00:32:13.450
All of those points are pulling on it with
the rubber band.

00:32:13.450 --> 00:32:16.049
And so it's going to end up in the middle.

00:32:16.049 --> 00:32:20.340
And it's trying to have the minimum total
tension in the rubber bands.

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:23.210
Let's suppose we really need X1 and X2.

00:32:23.210 --> 00:32:26.119
So rather than just a single line, there's
this plane.

00:32:26.119 --> 00:32:31.720
And the way I've tried to draw this is there's
a negative slope going down in the x2 direction

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:35.919
and a negative slope in the y direction, but
it slopes.

00:32:35.919 --> 00:32:38.399
You can see we've got clouds of points around
it.

00:32:38.399 --> 00:32:42.499
So suppose we really needed X1 and X2 to do
this.

00:32:42.499 --> 00:32:48.129
But if we only observe X1-- I'm going to back
up one slide-- it's like we're taking a flashlight

00:32:48.129 --> 00:32:50.799
shining it down on the X1 y plane.

00:32:50.799 --> 00:32:54.669
All those points are going to put their shadows
there.

00:32:54.669 --> 00:33:00.260
These gray lines I have-- the solid grey lines--
are the lines in my plane just to show the

00:33:00.260 --> 00:33:02.950
different groups, the different parts of X1
and X2.

00:33:02.950 --> 00:33:04.609
But you really wouldn't see that.

00:33:04.609 --> 00:33:09.049
And if you fit a regression line-- let's say
it's this dashed line here.

00:33:09.049 --> 00:33:12.650
It may be unbiased, but there's an awful lot
of noise around it as opposed to the amount

00:33:12.650 --> 00:33:14.299
of noise that would have been around the plane.

00:33:14.299 --> 00:33:18.119
So it might be unbiased but noisier.

00:33:18.119 --> 00:33:22.879
But suppose we didn't get to see all of the
X2s, and there was some change in whether

00:33:22.879 --> 00:33:24.279
they were included or not.

00:33:24.279 --> 00:33:31.799
So I'm only including the ones in the circle
now, which means for the low X1s, I only saw

00:33:31.799 --> 00:33:33.220
low X1s for the X2s.

00:33:33.220 --> 00:33:35.989
So the non-circled ones get left out.

00:33:35.989 --> 00:33:41.330
And if you notice the bar for X2 out here
is the higher values of X2.

00:33:41.330 --> 00:33:42.330
I've picked low ones.

00:33:42.330 --> 00:33:45.740
And for the higher values of X2, I picked
the high X1s.

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:49.309
Now, I would get a line that goes in the completely
other direction.

00:33:49.309 --> 00:33:51.559
So there's a sampling bit.

00:33:51.559 --> 00:33:56.260
If there are changes in the chance of things
being included, it can bias our results.

00:33:56.260 --> 00:33:58.210
And it can do it quite a bit.

00:33:58.210 --> 00:33:59.210
So what happened?

00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:03.059
It turned out X2 contained important information
we needed to consider.

00:34:03.059 --> 00:34:08.109
In this case, it had information about what
values of X1 were included in the dataset

00:34:08.109 --> 00:34:09.200
we got to see.

00:34:09.200 --> 00:34:13.840
And the result is a biased estimate of the
true association between X1 and y.

00:34:13.840 --> 00:34:17.250
So this is one type of bias that can result
in observational studies.

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:22.280
If we don't control for the value of X2, but
it had an influence on what we were seeing,

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:25.020
then our resulting estimates would be changed.

00:34:25.020 --> 00:34:29.140
Now this is, sort of, an example of confounding.

00:34:29.140 --> 00:34:31.230
Confounding is a big word in epidemiology.

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:35.070
And confounding gets brought up in big data
analysis often.

00:34:35.070 --> 00:34:40.740
Rothman and Greenland describe it as a confusion
of effects.

00:34:40.740 --> 00:34:46.840
In the example above, X2 was confounding the
effect of X1 because X2 was controlling which

00:34:46.840 --> 00:34:48.740
values of X1 we saw.

00:34:48.740 --> 00:34:53.200
Now confounding-- and I've written encyclopedia
entries on confounding.

00:34:53.200 --> 00:34:57.700
And every time I do, I get a headache because
it's not easy to define mathematically.

00:34:57.700 --> 00:35:04.230
There's no single mathematical definition
or statistical definition, so it's hard to

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:06.510
develop some sort of test or solution.

00:35:06.510 --> 00:35:09.760
But it is a conceptual mean we all understand.

00:35:09.760 --> 00:35:14.050
It's to an effect that would confound the
one we're interested in.

00:35:14.050 --> 00:35:19.160
So if we were looking at associations with
smoking and lung cancer, age might be a confounder.

00:35:19.160 --> 00:35:24.240
There may be a birth cohort confounding in
terms of level of smoking.

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:25.610
So the concept's familiar.

00:35:25.610 --> 00:35:29.250
There's always room for sensitivity analysis
with confounding.

00:35:29.250 --> 00:35:35.281
You can try to include potential confounders
and see whether your results change when you

00:35:35.281 --> 00:35:38.210
add them or take them out of your analysis.

00:35:38.210 --> 00:35:42.320
And I think this is a particularly important
contribution to the subject matter part of

00:35:42.320 --> 00:35:47.160
the Venn diagram for data science because
there are often established sets of potential

00:35:47.160 --> 00:35:49.120
confounders for particular outcomes.

00:35:49.120 --> 00:35:53.920
And ind epidemiology literature, if you don't
control for age and race, the referees are

00:35:53.920 --> 00:35:55.510
going to ask you why you didn't do it.

00:35:55.510 --> 00:35:58.500
So you could even check and not have it make
a difference and leave it out.

00:35:58.500 --> 00:36:02.690
You will still be asked about it because so
many health effects vary with age.

00:36:02.690 --> 00:36:07.400
So many health disparities are observed of
different race groups.

00:36:07.400 --> 00:36:10.600
And smoking has always had a big influence
too.

00:36:10.600 --> 00:36:13.980
So this is-- I'd encourage you to think about
it.

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:18.130
But, like I said, I've been thinking about
it for 30 years, and it still is hard to get

00:36:18.130 --> 00:36:20.840
a handle on.

00:36:20.840 --> 00:36:27.230
Now I need to click on-- there, we go.

00:36:27.230 --> 00:36:33.890
Another example of expression array-- and
this came up in Rafa Irizarry's BD2K webinar,

00:36:33.890 --> 00:36:34.890
which is archive.

00:36:34.890 --> 00:36:39.550
He was looking at gene expression arrays and
talking about batch effects.

00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:42.280
When gene expression arrays first came out,
it was an amazing thing.

00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:47.660
He could measure responsiveness in multiple
genes at once.

00:36:47.660 --> 00:36:52.230
And so his experiment here, he's discussing--
or the example he's talking about is-- suppose

00:36:52.230 --> 00:36:56.760
you're comparing gene expression array results,
and you have some of your gene expressions

00:36:56.760 --> 00:36:59.990
from tumor cells, and some of the genes are
from non-tumor cells.

00:36:59.990 --> 00:37:03.810
But you sent it to the lab in two batches,
and let's say they ran all the tumor samples

00:37:03.810 --> 00:37:06.030
on Friday, then everyone left for the weekend.

00:37:06.030 --> 00:37:08.650
They came back and ran the non-tumor cells
on Monday.

00:37:08.650 --> 00:37:12.580
And you see a difference in the expression
between the different genes you're investigating.

00:37:12.580 --> 00:37:13.580
What does it mean?

00:37:13.580 --> 00:37:18.340
Well, we'd like to think it means a tumor
or non-tumor difference, but it may well be

00:37:18.340 --> 00:37:19.870
a day of processing difference.

00:37:19.870 --> 00:37:24.620
It may take a while to get the lab set up
again on Monday, or we may not have things

00:37:24.620 --> 00:37:27.430
turned on and warmed up.

00:37:27.430 --> 00:37:31.850
Or-- this is a design issue behind even observational
studies.

00:37:31.850 --> 00:37:38.000
So one of the things you would see in the
evolution of publication about microarray

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:43.090
analysis or gene expression analysis was an
introduction of adding replicates and starting

00:37:43.090 --> 00:37:45.400
to look for some of these confounders.

00:37:45.400 --> 00:37:51.860
So if you're interested in and more of that
example, Rafa Irizarry's BD2K seminar does

00:37:51.860 --> 00:37:56.710
a nice job of walking through that and
showing a lot of examples.

00:37:56.710 --> 00:38:02.580
The last area I want to talk about is missing
data.

00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:05.560
So what if you're missing outcome values for
some predictors?

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:08.230
Or what if you're missing predictor values
for some outcomes?

00:38:08.230 --> 00:38:09.260
Should you ignore it?

00:38:09.260 --> 00:38:16.920
I mean, what becomes a frequent, if not ubiquitous,
issue in connecting large data sets is that

00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:19.990
you won't have all the pieces.

00:38:19.990 --> 00:38:20.990
Should you ignore it?

00:38:20.990 --> 00:38:22.410
Should you plug in a reasonable number?

00:38:22.410 --> 00:38:25.810
Should you just put in the mean and hope for
the best?

00:38:25.810 --> 00:38:30.920
These are all things that are done sometimes
and I think that we've all thought about.

00:38:30.920 --> 00:38:35.770
If you're missing the outcome, one algorithm
that's seen wide use on the smaller scale--

00:38:35.770 --> 00:38:41.440
and we have to see how it scales up-- is something
called the EM algorithm, the Expectation Maximization

00:38:41.440 --> 00:38:42.440
algorithm.

00:38:42.440 --> 00:38:46.710
So suppose you're missing a few of your outcomes
variables, but you have the Xs.

00:38:46.710 --> 00:38:48.820
You have the predictor values in your regression
model.

00:38:48.820 --> 00:38:50.730
So I'll show you some graphs of what this
looks like.

00:38:50.730 --> 00:38:55.900
The algorithm, or these algorithms, iterate
between filling in the missing information

00:38:55.900 --> 00:39:00.920
based on your current analysis-- your currently
fitted model-- and then refilling the model

00:39:00.920 --> 00:39:04.960
and updating your estimates, and so on until
this converges.

00:39:04.960 --> 00:39:09.770
So in a regression setting, we might have
the following.

00:39:09.770 --> 00:39:13.660
Let's say we have the observed dots, but the
two I've circled we didn't really get to see

00:39:13.660 --> 00:39:15.260
the y values.

00:39:15.260 --> 00:39:17.100
So I know what the x values are.

00:39:17.100 --> 00:39:20.710
Those are the dashed lines there, but I don't
know what the y value is.

00:39:20.710 --> 00:39:24.210
Now if I knew the right answer, I should get
the gray dashed line.

00:39:24.210 --> 00:39:27.020
But if I leave these two out, I get a bias.

00:39:27.020 --> 00:39:30.720
I get an orange graphed line.

00:39:30.720 --> 00:39:33.480
So the E-step is on the left, and the M-step
is on the right.

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:39.880
The E-step, you would assign the missing values--
the expectation from the orange line.

00:39:39.880 --> 00:39:44.060
This is what we think these two y values should
be if the model was right.

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:49.250
Then the M-step is to put those values in
and recalculate the orange lines, so it moves

00:39:49.250 --> 00:39:50.250
over.

00:39:50.250 --> 00:39:52.240
In this case, it got closer to the gray line.

00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:54.260
And then you'd iterate back and forth with
these.

00:39:54.260 --> 00:39:58.380
You'd be updating what you think y is an updating
what your estimate is.

00:39:58.380 --> 00:40:02.820
And another pretty broad conditions, this
will converge to the right answer.

00:40:02.820 --> 00:40:06.260
So again, just the E-step is expected values.

00:40:06.260 --> 00:40:11.920
The M-step is refit the model with updated
guesses for missing data, and you repeat til

00:40:11.920 --> 00:40:12.920
convergence.

00:40:12.920 --> 00:40:17.470
Now I'm sure this has been scaled to big data
and distributed computing.

00:40:17.470 --> 00:40:21.890
But trying to take the algorithm and break
it down in a way that could go off of distributed

00:40:21.890 --> 00:40:29.610
processors, and so on, it has a lot of validity
and a lot of interest.

00:40:29.610 --> 00:40:31.161
Now if the covariates are missing, what do
we do?

00:40:31.161 --> 00:40:32.520
Do we ignore them?

00:40:32.520 --> 00:40:37.130
We've already seen that ignoring X2 in our
example earlier lead to confounding and bias

00:40:37.130 --> 00:40:38.760
because it was important.

00:40:38.760 --> 00:40:41.840
Could we impute a value or make a guess?

00:40:41.840 --> 00:40:43.550
What if we could guess a lot of values?

00:40:43.550 --> 00:40:45.420
Would there would be value in that?

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:52.340
Well, multiple imputation is an area that's
been used a lot in smaller data analysis and

00:40:52.340 --> 00:40:54.450
has some similarities to
cross validation.

00:40:54.450 --> 00:40:58.490
It gets used a lot in big data analysis.

00:40:58.490 --> 00:41:01.570
It's just not-- they're not doing exactly
the same thing.

00:41:01.570 --> 00:41:07.610
What multiple imputation does is for every
missing value, you simulate multiple values.

00:41:07.610 --> 00:41:14.640
And then-- even doing two or three for each
one rather than just one allows you to estimate

00:41:14.640 --> 00:41:18.300
the missing value itself and the variability
in that missing value.

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:22.880
So you're not guessing it perfectly, but you're
going to have to guess it with some variation

00:41:22.880 --> 00:41:26.600
and then percolate that variation through
to your model.

00:41:26.600 --> 00:41:32.130
So the imputed values inform us on the overall
variability of our estimate or prediction,

00:41:32.130 --> 00:41:37.410
and they take into account the extra variability
we've added by putting our imputation in.

00:41:37.410 --> 00:41:39.040
So this requires a model for imputation.

00:41:39.040 --> 00:41:40.250
How would you impute it?

00:41:40.250 --> 00:41:42.800
How often would you impute?

00:41:42.800 --> 00:41:47.850
And the Little and Rubin book is really the
standard for both EM algorithm and multiple

00:41:47.850 --> 00:41:48.850
imputation.

00:41:48.850 --> 00:41:52.440
I also like Schafer's book, even though it's
been out quite a while.

00:41:52.440 --> 00:41:55.280
There may be a second edition to this.

00:41:55.280 --> 00:41:59.670
And seeing how some of these ideas could be
played out in the big data setting, I think,

00:41:59.670 --> 00:42:02.430
would be incredibly valuable.

00:42:02.430 --> 00:42:07.770
Just to wrap up, and then we'll leave us some
time for questions, if any have come in.

00:42:07.770 --> 00:42:11.550
Multiple testing-- the main issue is if we
check a lot of things, we need to control

00:42:11.550 --> 00:42:13.850
for the impact of false positives.

00:42:13.850 --> 00:42:14.890
So what do we do about this?

00:42:14.890 --> 00:42:16.490
We should always worry about multiple testing.

00:42:16.490 --> 00:42:21.380
I mean, I don't say you should not do it,
but you should be somewhat concerned and maybe

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:23.120
feel a little bad about it.

00:42:23.120 --> 00:42:28.460
But, you know, the ideas like false discovery
rate and similar summaries are gaining a lot

00:42:28.460 --> 00:42:29.680
of ground.

00:42:29.680 --> 00:42:32.950
We've got a better understanding of them.

00:42:32.950 --> 00:42:39.130
And it involved backing up to the basic ideas
and building something that was more extendable.

00:42:39.130 --> 00:42:42.680
And bias-- the issue is the estimates differ
from the true values.

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:45.520
And what we should do, well, of course, we
should worry about that.

00:42:45.520 --> 00:42:49.870
We should document and adjust if possible
as my ear and mouth thermometer example.

00:42:49.870 --> 00:42:54.490
But also trying to get-- in a more general
sense-- trying to model the observation process,

00:42:54.490 --> 00:42:58.220
starting to think about how we see the data
we see-- how that can have an influence on

00:42:58.220 --> 00:42:59.220
this.

00:42:59.220 --> 00:43:03.790
So I really think that subject matter component
is important in data science.

00:43:03.790 --> 00:43:08.120
It's not just a calculation and then hope
the numbers are right.

00:43:08.120 --> 00:43:12.000
So I think there's a lot of work to do on
thinking about bias.

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:18.170
And, again, tied into that Bayesian estimation,
it provides a way to borrow information that

00:43:18.170 --> 00:43:22.040
may be a little biased, but it may improve
our precision.

00:43:22.040 --> 00:43:26.190
With confounding, we've got to factor outside
of our analysis impacting our results.

00:43:26.190 --> 00:43:29.010
Again, what we should do is worry.

00:43:29.010 --> 00:43:35.440
And epidemiology articles are particularly
good at thinking about ways that the observational

00:43:35.440 --> 00:43:40.560
conclusions may be wrong and how to prove
it better.

00:43:40.560 --> 00:43:46.010
I think sensitivity analyses are always a
good idea in terms of checking the impact

00:43:46.010 --> 00:43:47.010
of things.

00:43:47.010 --> 00:43:51.720
Now on a large data setting, we might not
have the flexibility for, say, simulations

00:43:51.720 --> 00:43:56.810
that we do on a smaller scale, but we might
want to think about ways to look for evidence.

00:43:56.810 --> 00:44:02.560
You know, what wake does confounding leave
in your data as it moves through it?

00:44:02.560 --> 00:44:06.570
It's also important to catalogue the common
confounders in a certain area of application.

00:44:06.570 --> 00:44:10.690
We shouldn't be doing analyses de novo in
every setting, and we should learn from the

00:44:10.690 --> 00:44:11.690
past.

00:44:11.690 --> 00:44:15.270
I think this is one of the points that the
Lazer et al. article was making about the

00:44:15.270 --> 00:44:17.750
Flu Trends.

00:44:17.750 --> 00:44:22.200
It's fine to make mistakes, but we also don't
need to do our analysis completely oblivious

00:44:22.200 --> 00:44:26.220
of what people know about this area or about
the data types.

00:44:26.220 --> 00:44:29.440
Finally, with missing data, the issue is the
data are not complete.

00:44:29.440 --> 00:44:31.620
This is not a surprise to anyone.

00:44:31.620 --> 00:44:35.940
But, again, we should worry about it not just
brush it under the rug.

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:40.820
Especially documenting, the reason it's missing--
I gave you an example with my confounder,

00:44:40.820 --> 00:44:45.270
where I had missing datas on X2 that impacted
the estimate of X1.

00:44:45.270 --> 00:44:50.130
And that's because the probability of being
included was related to the outcome I was

00:44:50.130 --> 00:44:51.960
looking at.

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:56.210
You have a lot less bias if that missingness
mechanism is random.

00:44:56.210 --> 00:45:02.780
If you have data missing completely at random
or a little more generally missing at random,

00:45:02.780 --> 00:45:05.360
it won't impact your results as much.

00:45:05.360 --> 00:45:09.400
There are things like the EM algorithm and
imputation, but I do think there's a lot of

00:45:09.400 --> 00:45:13.930
work there to think about scaling those up
into more general tools and having them fit

00:45:13.930 --> 00:45:17.170
in to the data scientist toolbox.

00:45:17.170 --> 00:45:22.940
And model missingness-- I think there's a
lot of room for explaining why the data are

00:45:22.940 --> 00:45:23.940
missing.

00:45:23.940 --> 00:45:26.440
That may actually be the first round of analysis
you do.

00:45:26.440 --> 00:45:34.700
In my example, there was an indication that
the value of X2 had an impact on whether X1

00:45:34.700 --> 00:45:35.740
was observed.

00:45:35.740 --> 00:45:42.890
And if you can understand then model the missingness,
this might help untangle the effects it has.

00:45:42.890 --> 00:45:48.770
So to just bring this all together-- data
and analysis issues that come from statistics

00:45:48.770 --> 00:45:54.350
and epidemiology by statistics, like the four
we talked about today, all have deep backgrounds

00:45:54.350 --> 00:45:58.660
at standard size analysis, but they may not
scale directly to the big data setting.

00:45:58.660 --> 00:46:04.180
And I think this is a very interesting area
for collaborations between the large-scale

00:46:04.180 --> 00:46:11.110
data scientist interests and these people
trained in statistics and epidemiology.

00:46:11.110 --> 00:46:12.880
So these aren't issues that one can avoid.

00:46:12.880 --> 00:46:16.680
So I wasn't being totally facetious about
saying worry about everything.

00:46:16.680 --> 00:46:19.250
I think worry is essential to doing good work.

00:46:19.250 --> 00:46:24.730
The Bonferroni and false discovery rate example
illustrates how addressing data issues in

00:46:24.730 --> 00:46:28.580
a big data setting can require rethinking
even basic assumptions.

00:46:28.580 --> 00:46:30.330
It didn't say the assumptions were wrong.

00:46:30.330 --> 00:46:35.190
It just said the relevant proportion we were
interested in was different than what the

00:46:35.190 --> 00:46:37.720
Bonferroni had been adjusting.

00:46:37.720 --> 00:46:41.650
So there's a lot of background within each
of the three or seven or however many elements

00:46:41.650 --> 00:46:42.650
of the Venn diagram.

00:46:42.650 --> 00:46:46.810
But I think the most interesting research
advances occur in the overlap.

00:46:46.810 --> 00:46:51.320
And that's where I think data science will
come into its own.

00:46:51.320 --> 00:46:56.000
So I really like the idea of team data science
and having different skill sets available

00:46:56.000 --> 00:46:58.840
and has a great framework for addressing this.

00:46:58.840 --> 00:47:02.380
I think it's more interesting to have a statistician
and a computer scientist in the room both

00:47:02.380 --> 00:47:06.170
thinking about the same problem rather than
having them work separately and then present

00:47:06.170 --> 00:47:08.360
their solutions to each other.

00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:11.680
So I would say let's go forward and make new
mistakes together.

00:47:11.680 --> 00:47:16.720
And I believe we have some time for questions.

00:47:16.720 --> 00:47:18.140
Fantastic lecture, Lance.

00:47:18.140 --> 00:47:19.420
Thank you so much.

00:47:19.420 --> 00:47:24.180
We do want to encourage people to write in
their questions.

00:47:24.180 --> 00:47:28.460
And as people are putting fingers to keys,
I had a couple of questions.

00:47:28.460 --> 00:47:32.260
And I thought these are, sort of, big questions.

00:47:32.260 --> 00:47:36.100
And I wanted to just get your thoughts on
these.

00:47:36.100 --> 00:47:41.230
You had mentioned one thing in some of your
introductory remarks in a couple of remarks

00:47:41.230 --> 00:47:42.230
you had made.

00:47:42.230 --> 00:47:46.690
And one of them involved an interesting term,
which I thought was-- I don't know-- caught

00:47:46.690 --> 00:47:47.690
my attention.

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:50.980
That was of data hubris-- big data hubris.

00:47:50.980 --> 00:47:56.020
And you can understand that lots of people
think, oh, if we just have more data, the

00:47:56.020 --> 00:47:57.200
answers will be in there.

00:47:57.200 --> 00:48:01.500
And we've got all these methods and techniques
to go and just ferret them out, and we'll

00:48:01.500 --> 00:48:03.350
just know everything.

00:48:03.350 --> 00:48:07.310
And the hubris element of that I find to be
somewhat fascinating.

00:48:07.310 --> 00:48:12.630
I think one of the more recent examples of
this that everybody's aware of-- and I don't

00:48:12.630 --> 00:48:19.020
mean to get political here, but-- was in the
recent US elections, where it was clear that

00:48:19.020 --> 00:48:23.690
one candidate, everyone was predicting that
that candidate was totally going to walk away

00:48:23.690 --> 00:48:25.070
with the election.

00:48:25.070 --> 00:48:30.520
And then, on the given night, everyone was
a little surprised at how that turned out.

00:48:30.520 --> 00:48:37.790
And I'm curious if you have any thoughts about
whether or not there was any bias or variance

00:48:37.790 --> 00:48:41.970
in the processes that led up to that somewhat
surprising outcome.

00:48:41.970 --> 00:48:50.500
Well, I'd say the kind of ubiquity and ease
of doing a survey that may not be a scientific

00:48:50.500 --> 00:48:54.510
sample survey-- I think the polls, and so
on, really took a hit.

00:48:54.510 --> 00:48:59.280
I don't know if other people are like me,
but I'm getting multiple calls at dinner time

00:48:59.280 --> 00:49:04.840
now that are recorded surveys on how's the
government doing.

00:49:04.840 --> 00:49:07.980
And in my heart, as a statistician, I want
to supply data.

00:49:07.980 --> 00:49:12.480
But in my head, I also realize these are all
paid for by different groups.

00:49:12.480 --> 00:49:17.420
And the wording of the questions are looking
one way or the other.

00:49:17.420 --> 00:49:23.310
And so I think that it was a lot of-- with
the diversified news media and everything,

00:49:23.310 --> 00:49:25.540
they know what will play to their audience.

00:49:25.540 --> 00:49:27.840
And so questions were asked in a certain way.

00:49:27.840 --> 00:49:37.740
I don't think there was as much pooling of
the information across the survey.

00:49:37.740 --> 00:49:39.760
I mean, there's a very complicated setting.

00:49:39.760 --> 00:49:45.870
There's a lot written about Facebook and the
kind of divergent-- you have your group of

00:49:45.870 --> 00:49:48.650
friends, and you forward articles that tell
you the same thing.

00:49:48.650 --> 00:49:51.110
We're all in our own bubbles.

00:49:51.110 --> 00:49:54.180
I think FiveThirtyEight tries to do a good
job of that.

00:49:54.180 --> 00:49:55.680
But you've got all this information.

00:49:55.680 --> 00:50:00.390
FiveThirtyEight worked really well the last
couple of elections because it was based on

00:50:00.390 --> 00:50:03.890
polls that had measures of uncertainty.

00:50:03.890 --> 00:50:06.090
And that's what it's good at doing, putting
it together.

00:50:06.090 --> 00:50:08.950
But if the
questions are leading, your probability and

00:50:08.950 --> 00:50:13.520
my probability of participating is not equal
for every poll.

00:50:13.520 --> 00:50:20.570
And I am frustrated by that, but I also am
reacting just like a lot of other people are.

00:50:20.570 --> 00:50:25.170
I may only be answering polls that cater to
what I hope.

00:50:25.170 --> 00:50:28.960
And I think in that setting, a poll doesn't
work anymore.

00:50:28.960 --> 00:50:33.570
So there is this idea that having a lot of
data, but finding that it's the people who

00:50:33.570 --> 00:50:34.570
responded.

00:50:34.570 --> 00:50:38.380
And that's why modeling the missingness is
really important and understanding where it

00:50:38.380 --> 00:50:39.910
comes from.

00:50:39.910 --> 00:50:46.840
So going back to that data analysis whirling
vortex, knowing what data you would like to

00:50:46.840 --> 00:50:50.640
get and how that differs from the data that
you can get or you ultimately get and what

00:50:50.640 --> 00:50:54.560
question you did answer with your data,
I think we assumed we we're answering the

00:50:54.560 --> 00:50:56.270
question about who's going to win the election.

00:50:56.270 --> 00:51:02.320
But we were actually answering questions about
who's ahead in the minds of those who respond

00:51:02.320 --> 00:51:03.320
to this particular.

00:51:03.320 --> 00:51:07.350
Once we start putting all the caveats in there,
we start realizing we answered a much more

00:51:07.350 --> 00:51:12.260
specific question than the general one we
were interested in.

00:51:12.260 --> 00:51:18.950
So one of our listeners writes in, and they're
asking about the expectation maximization

00:51:18.950 --> 00:51:25.150
methodology and whether or not the iterations
that you use to fill in the missing data--

00:51:25.150 --> 00:51:27.960
how often does that lead to a wrong model?

00:51:27.960 --> 00:51:28.960
Does that make sense?

00:51:28.960 --> 00:51:29.960
And so-
Yeah.

00:51:29.960 --> 00:51:31.280
Why not just ignore those points?

00:51:31.280 --> 00:51:35.340
You go through this big thing, and it converges
on something, and you think, oh, Eureka!

00:51:35.340 --> 00:51:36.340
There's my model.

00:51:36.340 --> 00:51:37.340
But, in fact, it's, in fact, wrong.

00:51:37.340 --> 00:51:38.460
How often does that happen?

00:51:38.460 --> 00:51:44.180
Right, so it has a good-- it has a pretty
general set of regularity conditions, the

00:51:44.180 --> 00:51:48.620
conditions under which it does what it's supposed
to do and converges to the right answer.

00:51:48.620 --> 00:51:53.220
It won't converge to as precise an answer
than if you had all the data, because there's

00:51:53.220 --> 00:51:55.870
some uncertainty in those missing pieces.

00:51:55.870 --> 00:51:58.340
But it should move in the right direction.

00:51:58.340 --> 00:52:02.160
There's a fair amount of literature on the--
just for lack of a better term, calling it

00:52:02.160 --> 00:52:06.110
a small data setting, of its performance and
how well it works.

00:52:06.110 --> 00:52:10.560
You know, I think one of the biggest questions
for some of these statistical techniques and

00:52:10.560 --> 00:52:16.540
rolling them out to the big data setting is
could you distribute it and put it back together?

00:52:16.540 --> 00:52:24.220
So it's like MapReduce, except it's doing
the analysis on that.

00:52:24.220 --> 00:52:31.240
So that's how there's-- used to be-- it had
a different name, but Bill Chambers is doing

00:52:31.240 --> 00:52:36.070
a lot of this-- of what kind of statistical
algorithms can be broken into pieces that

00:52:36.070 --> 00:52:40.230
they can be sent off to different processors
and then recombined in such a way that you

00:52:40.230 --> 00:52:41.230
keep the answer?

00:52:41.230 --> 00:52:44.670
So I think that's a very active area for work.

00:52:44.670 --> 00:52:46.670
It's wide open for good ideas.

00:52:46.670 --> 00:52:52.370
And, I think, that's an area where pairing
a statistical researcher or a methodologist

00:52:52.370 --> 00:52:57.670
with a computer scientist could lead to some
big improvements in both areas.

00:52:57.670 --> 00:53:04.090
And the joint improvement of both areas is
where data science gets better.

00:53:04.090 --> 00:53:06.000
Great.

00:53:06.000 --> 00:53:12.830
Another question I had was-- I see amongst
a lot of younger investigators, some of the

00:53:12.830 --> 00:53:23.010
post-doctoral Fellows that I've encountered,
there seems to be a particular 

00:53:23.010 --> 00:53:27.820
value put on finding ever more smaller P-values.

00:53:27.820 --> 00:53:33.480
And so somebody will go find something that's
1 times 10 to the negative 16th and get very,

00:53:33.480 --> 00:53:38.970
very excited, because, oh, it's much better
than 1 times 10 to the negative 4th.

00:53:38.970 --> 00:53:40.200
And so they think it's more important.

00:53:40.200 --> 00:53:45.490
And they make the classic mistake, I think,
of mistaking statistical significance with

00:53:45.490 --> 00:53:48.310
practical and theoretical significance.

00:53:48.310 --> 00:53:52.950
They may only be accounting for 3% of the
variance, but is wildly significant because

00:53:52.950 --> 00:53:54.880
they've got big data.

00:53:54.880 --> 00:54:00.630
What sort of message can we convey to people
to maybe avoid some of the issues that come

00:54:00.630 --> 00:54:03.480
up when you fall into that sort of trap, if
that makes sense?

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:07.300
Yeah, I mean, I think the easy trap to fall
in with P-values is the smaller one must be

00:54:07.300 --> 00:54:08.970
more significant.

00:54:08.970 --> 00:54:13.200
You know, one of the things-- there's been
some push back.

00:54:13.200 --> 00:54:15.200
Some journals want except P-values anymore.

00:54:15.200 --> 00:54:21.220
And I think part of that is because there's--
I think P-values serve a purpose by themselves,

00:54:21.220 --> 00:54:26.070
but I think they're overused and overinterpreted,
and this is one example in which that happens.

00:54:26.070 --> 00:54:31.590
You know, even if the null hypothesis is true,
the actual P-value you get should be uniformly

00:54:31.590 --> 00:54:34.070
distributed between 0 and 1.

00:54:34.070 --> 00:54:41.630
So ranking them if you're not rejecting doesn't
make sense, and then the distribution shifts.

00:54:41.630 --> 00:54:43.630
I'm saying a lot of words.

00:54:43.630 --> 00:54:50.160
What I think would be helpful is if we start
thinking about measures of association and

00:54:50.160 --> 00:54:53.800
then bringing in the subject matter aspect
of the Venn diagram.

00:54:53.800 --> 00:54:56.700
Is the difference we saw really meaningful?

00:54:56.700 --> 00:55:02.480
Because there can be statistical significance,
but the difference you actually detected was

00:55:02.480 --> 00:55:04.340
kind of small.

00:55:04.340 --> 00:55:08.740
And I don't like to see these P-value contests
like you said.

00:55:08.740 --> 00:55:12.830
I think that's putting too much emphasis on
something that's hard to estimate.

00:55:12.830 --> 00:55:18.090
And, to me, a P-value of 10 to the minus 6--
I don't even know what that means, because

00:55:18.090 --> 00:55:23.000
I've never really observed too many things
with a million observations to get this one

00:55:23.000 --> 00:55:24.000
in a million.

00:55:24.000 --> 00:55:30.780
And if you get these really tiny ones, I think
weird things happen.

00:55:30.780 --> 00:55:37.090
And that we don't appreciate that even though
it had a small probability, it still could

00:55:37.090 --> 00:55:38.090
happen.

00:55:38.090 --> 00:55:39.190
Yeah, I agree.

00:55:39.190 --> 00:55:47.190
I find that when people in certain areas of
big data research start talking about P-values

00:55:47.190 --> 00:55:52.700
that are 10 to the minus 16th, or something,
I mean, they're down in the machine precision

00:55:52.700 --> 00:55:55.110
of errors.

00:55:55.110 --> 00:55:58.190
You just can't trust anything that gets that
low.

00:55:58.190 --> 00:56:02.500
And the emphasis being on the P-value, I think,
is somewhat misguided.

00:56:02.500 --> 00:56:07.030
And I'm not quite sure how that happened because
I was always brought up to think, how much

00:56:07.030 --> 00:56:09.090
of the variance am i accounting for, for example.

00:56:09.090 --> 00:56:12.260
You know, that givers me a sense of how strong
my model is.

00:56:12.260 --> 00:56:15.630
And that ends up not being very important
to some people.

00:56:15.630 --> 00:56:18.340
Maybe I missed a memo, but it seems that that's
what people-

00:56:18.340 --> 00:56:23.750
I think it's an example of data science is
a new field with people coming from other

00:56:23.750 --> 00:56:24.750
fields.

00:56:24.750 --> 00:56:28.500
And that's why I was talking about the team
data science, because these concepts that

00:56:28.500 --> 00:56:31.620
you carry with you are what you trained to
look for.

00:56:31.620 --> 00:56:34.650
What was important about doing the analysis?

00:56:34.650 --> 00:56:39.410
And having people with different perspectives,
I think, is in a broader image of that.

00:56:39.410 --> 00:56:45.600
And so there's people who think all of statistics
is what they get in one class.

00:56:45.600 --> 00:56:49.550
Similarly, there's people who think all of
computer science is what they got in one class.

00:56:49.550 --> 00:56:52.560
And we really need to recognize that's not
the case.

00:56:52.560 --> 00:56:56.030
And it's very hard to be an expert at both
of them.

00:56:56.030 --> 00:57:01.850
But you can learn a lot of things from experts
in them if they're getting together and working

00:57:01.850 --> 00:57:02.850
together.

00:57:02.850 --> 00:57:04.430
So I think data science is a highly collaborative
field.

00:57:04.430 --> 00:57:09.500
I like to think of statistics as highly collaborative
but data science, I think, is often even more

00:57:09.500 --> 00:57:10.500
so.

00:57:10.500 --> 00:57:17.620
And it's a combination of people with different
intuitions, expertise, experience, default

00:57:17.620 --> 00:57:18.750
assumptions.

00:57:18.750 --> 00:57:23.080
And that's where I think we can sort a lot
of things out with these kind of collaborations

00:57:23.080 --> 00:57:24.190
and conversations.

00:57:24.190 --> 00:57:30.670
I'm really glad to hear you say that, because
the last question I had was how can we help

00:57:30.670 --> 00:57:38.840
universities and university administrators
understand that team data science is important?

00:57:38.840 --> 00:57:40.640
It's not just statistics.

00:57:40.640 --> 00:57:44.550
It's not just computer science that most universities
already have.

00:57:44.550 --> 00:57:46.990
But it's something that's unique and special.

00:57:46.990 --> 00:57:50.880
How can we help them to recognize that?

00:57:50.880 --> 00:57:55.750
I think it's good to highlight the successes
in places that have-- a lot of universities

00:57:55.750 --> 00:57:58.380
develop institutes of data science, for instance.

00:57:58.380 --> 00:58:02.320
Some of those institutes are within computer
science, or they're within another field,

00:58:02.320 --> 00:58:07.140
but I think the really strongest ones and
the ones that I think are making the biggest

00:58:07.140 --> 00:58:11.280
difference will ultimately-- and I hope this
is the case-- continue to make a big difference

00:58:11.280 --> 00:58:15.010
will be the ones that have this interdisciplinary
feel.

00:58:15.010 --> 00:58:20.340
And I have gone to a lot of statistics conferences
where people say, well, aren't we data scientists?

00:58:20.340 --> 00:58:21.850
That was the early conversation.

00:58:21.850 --> 00:58:27.810
The conversation I heard last week at a statistics
conference was we're part of an Institute,

00:58:27.810 --> 00:58:34.990
and we have a track on statistical methodology
or a track on health analytics because we

00:58:34.990 --> 00:58:39.750
work in a lot of health area, we can help
put the algorithms in perspective and what

00:58:39.750 --> 00:58:41.170
kind of questions they can answer.

00:58:41.170 --> 00:58:48.290
And we establish some-- not standards but
usual ways of doing things.

00:58:48.290 --> 00:58:55.860
To me, I think that's a big development because
that would argue against having a center for

00:58:55.860 --> 00:59:00.080
data science inside a statistics department
or a center for data science inside a computer

00:59:00.080 --> 00:59:01.940
science department without talking to each
other.

00:59:01.940 --> 00:59:09.080
So, you know, I think as we see developments
happen that have benefited from the interdisciplinary

00:59:09.080 --> 00:59:15.390
collaboration, I think that's where we'll
really-- I hate to wait for that to happen.

00:59:15.390 --> 00:59:19.800
But I think that's where the big developments
will be.

00:59:19.800 --> 00:59:26.160
And so as those happen-- and also, I am a
little afraid that some of the purely algorithmic

00:59:26.160 --> 00:59:28.630
approaches are going to make some big mistakes
somewhere.

00:59:28.630 --> 00:59:31.300
And maybe it's not just guessing how many
people had flu last

00:59:31.300 --> 00:59:32.300
week.

00:59:32.300 --> 00:59:38.180
Maybe it's predicting a drug assignment for
cancer in precision medicine.

00:59:38.180 --> 00:59:43.390
If you use a machine learning algorithm that
works really well for getting pretty good

00:59:43.390 --> 00:59:49.010
increase in your profit in a business setting,
and you apply that blindly as a black box--

00:59:49.010 --> 00:59:53.270
and I don't know if anyone would do that--
but suppose you did to defining that, you

00:59:53.270 --> 00:59:57.880
may get 90% of the things right, but you've
got 10% of patients who are getting the wrong

00:59:57.880 --> 00:59:58.880
dose.

00:59:58.880 --> 00:59:59.880
And that could be fatal.

00:59:59.880 --> 01:00:01.320
It could be costly.

01:00:01.320 --> 01:00:07.940
So some of this is-- I think statisticians
are inbred worriers because of variance.

01:00:07.940 --> 01:00:10.530
And we think about all the kind of errors
we can make.

01:00:10.530 --> 01:00:12.930
We give them names-- type 1, type 2 errors.

01:00:12.930 --> 01:00:15.460
We need to develop what can go wrong.

01:00:15.460 --> 01:00:18.730
And that's where these four topics came up
today.

01:00:18.730 --> 01:00:25.050
Well, let me thank you on behalf of all of
our listeners today for your fantastic talk,

01:00:25.050 --> 01:00:26.050
Dr. Waller.

01:00:26.050 --> 01:00:27.290
Thank you very, very much.

01:00:27.290 --> 01:00:34.250
Once again, we'll be having another one of
these next Friday at 9:00 am Pacific and noon

01:00:34.250 --> 01:00:35.250
Eastern.

01:00:35.250 --> 01:00:37.890
And we look forward to seeing you all again
then.

01:00:37.890 --> 01:00:39.150
Thank you again, Dr. Waller.

01:00:39.150 --> 01:00:43.289
Thank you very much.

